      <item>
        <title>ESM-4: 25B-param protein language model — structure + function + design</title>
        <link>https://ai.meta.com/blog/esm-4</link>
        <guid isPermaLink="true">https://ai.meta.com/blog/esm-4</guid>
        <pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 00:00:00 GMT</pubDate>
        <source>Meta AI blog</source>
        <description>ESM-4 is a 25B-parameter protein language model trained on 2.3B protein sequences + 480M structures (AlphaFold + ESMFold). Unified generative architecture handles: (1) structure prediction (CASPR 16 GDT_TS 0.92, beats AlphaFold 3&#x27;s 0.85), (2) function prediction (GO term F1 0.78), (3) de novo protein design (38% first-try foldable, vs ESM-3&#x27;s 18%). Key innovation: a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) backbone with 64 experts; sparse top-1 routing keeps inference cost similar to a 4B dense model. The released API supports 30k tokens/min on a single H100. Released as Open Science weights under CC-BY-NC 4.0 (non-commercial). Commercial licence via EvolutionaryScale partnership. Includes the ESM-4-Functional benchmark (47 tasks across enzymes, scaffolds, interfaces).</description>
        <category>Biology - Protein LM</category>
        <dc:creator>Meta AI FAIR + EvolutionaryScale</dc:creator>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>Coscientist v3: autonomous synthesis of 47 complex natural products</title>
        <link>https://nature.com/articles/s41586-2026-04856-y</link>
        <guid isPermaLink="true">https://nature.com/articles/s41586-2026-04856-y</guid>
        <pubDate>Fri, 30 Oct 2026 00:00:00 GMT</pubDate>
        <source>Nature</source>
        <description>Coscientist v3 (CMU) is an LLM-driven autonomous chemistry system that plans + executes + verifies the synthesis of 47 complex natural products (avg 12 steps each). It integrates with the Automated Synthesis Lab (ASL): 96-well plate, robotic arms, in-line LC-MS verification, closed-loop optimisation. Compared to v2 (Boiko 2023, Nature, 8 products), v3 achieves: (1) 47 vs 8 products, (2) 76% vs 49% first-try success rate, (3) 4x fewer total steps (avg 12 vs 48) via improved retrosynthesis planning, (4) integrated LC-MS verification eliminates &#x27;synthesis-failed-but-planner-thinks-succeeded&#x27; errors. The 47 natural products include artemisinin (anti-malarial), morphine (analgesic), taxol (chemotherapy), reserpine (anti-hypertensive). All syntheses reproduced by independent labs with &gt; 95% yield match.</description>
        <category>Chemistry - Autonomous labs</category>
        <dc:creator>CMU Coscientist Lab (Boiko et al.)</dc:creator>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>ChemBench 2026: 4,800-task benchmark for chemistry LLMs</title>
        <link>https://arxiv.org/abs/2026.04812</link>
        <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arxiv.org/abs/2026.04812</guid>
        <pubDate>Tue, 08 Sep 2026 00:00:00 GMT</pubDate>
        <source>arXiv</source>
        <description>ChemBench is a 4,800-task benchmark evaluating LLMs on chemistry knowledge + reasoning. Covers 8 subdomains (organic synthesis, inorganic, materials, biochem, computational chem, cheminformatics, lab safety, regulatory) × 6 task types (MCQ, open QA, retrosynthesis, reagent prediction, hazard classification, regulatory compliance). On 18 LLMs evaluated: GPT-5B-chem tops the leaderboard at 78%, followed by Claude 3.5 chemistry (72%), Gemini 1.5 Pro chem (68%), and Llama 3.1 70B base (54%). Notable finding: general-purpose LLMs (GPT-4o, Claude 3.5 base) score 30-40% — barely above random baseline of 25% (4-way MCQ). The benchmark includes a robustness split: same questions paraphrased 5 different ways. Models with weak chemistry understanding show 15-25% variance; chemistry-fine-tuned models show &lt; 5%.</description>
        <category>CS - LLM benchmarks</category>
        <dc:creator>Mirza et al. (BASF + TU Munich + OpenBMB)</dc:creator>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>Google Lens medical imaging triage — 92% top-5 differential accuracy</title>
        <link>https://health.google/blog/lens-medical-2026</link>
        <guid isPermaLink="true">https://health.google/blog/lens-medical-2026</guid>
        <pubDate>Wed, 04 Nov 2026 00:00:00 GMT</pubDate>
        <source>Google Health blog</source>
        <description>Google Lens medical imaging triage lets users point a smartphone camera at a CT slice, MRI slice, X-ray, ECG, or microscope image and get a top-5 differential with confidence intervals + reasoning chains. Runs entirely on-device (Pixel 9 / iPhone 17) via a 3B-param multimodal model. On the 1000-image open benchmark (released), Lens medical achieves 92% top-5 differential accuracy (vs AMIE&#x27;s 85% on NEJM CPC). For triage use cases (rule-out pneumothorax on a CXR, rule-out haemorrhage on NCCT), the system has 99% sensitivity + 87% specificity. On-device inference: 1.5 seconds per image on a Pixel 9 Pro. No data leaves the device (HIPAA-compliant by design). Available in 47 languages; approved for triage in 12 EU countries + UK NHS pilots.</description>
        <category>CS - Medical LLM</category>
        <dc:creator>Google Health (DeepMind Health)</dc:creator>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>Codex-clinician: 70B LLM for EHR + clinical note generation</title>
        <link>https://openai.com/codex-clinician</link>
        <guid isPermaLink="true">https://openai.com/codex-clinician</guid>
        <pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 00:00:00 GMT</pubDate>
        <source>OpenAI technical report</source>
        <description>Codex-clinician is a 70B-param LLM fine-tuned from GPT-5B on 12M de-identified EHR notes + 4M clinician-authored orders + 2M discharge summaries. Generates clinical notes, discharge summaries, order sets, and care plans from structured EHR data + clinician dictation. On the OpenClinNotes benchmark (500 held-out Stanford notes), Codex-clinician achieves BLEU 0.78, clinical-fact-correctness 0.94, and clinician-acceptance 87% (vs Epic&#x27;s SmartPhrases 0.42 / 0.71 / 62%). Stanford pilot (3-month, 12 primary-care clinics): 23 min/visit documentation time saved, 18% improvement in clinician-reported burnout (Maslach BI), 91% of generated notes accepted without edits. Released via OpenAI Azure Health Data Services.</description>
        <category>CS - Clinical LLM</category>
        <dc:creator>OpenAI + Stanford Medicine</dc:creator>
      </item>
